WEBVTT

00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:45.840
This is the hut of Cape Evans where Captain Scott and his party spent the winter of 1911.

00:01:45.840 --> 00:01:50.160
The freezing Antarctic temperatures have kept everything exactly as it was.

00:01:50.160 --> 00:01:56.680
Food, equipment, and perhaps most poignant of all, clothing and bedding on the bunks.

00:01:56.680 --> 00:02:00.680
As though the explorers left just yesterday.

00:02:00.680 --> 00:02:07.760
And this is how it was around that same table on June the 6th 1911, Scott's 43rd birthday.

00:02:07.760 --> 00:02:12.760
He and his team were spending the winter here so as to be ready as soon as the sun reappeared

00:02:12.760 --> 00:02:16.040
to start the trek to the Pole.

00:02:16.040 --> 00:02:19.720
They lightened the long dark days with their own entertainment.

00:02:19.720 --> 00:02:22.320
But these were serious minded men.

00:02:22.320 --> 00:02:26.000
For some of them reaching the Pole was of secondary importance.

00:02:26.000 --> 00:02:32.760
They had come to make scientific discoveries in geology, biology, glaciology, meteorology.

00:02:32.760 --> 00:02:39.520
And they had a surprisingly well equipped laboratory.

00:02:39.520 --> 00:02:45.960
And that is still here too.

00:02:45.960 --> 00:02:49.360
Photography was in the hands of Herbert Ponting.

00:02:49.360 --> 00:02:52.360
He took cine film as well as still photographs.

00:02:52.360 --> 00:03:00.000
And he had his own cramped darkroom in which to develop and print his huge glass plates.

00:03:00.000 --> 00:03:03.320
They had with them large stocks of tin food.

00:03:03.320 --> 00:03:08.400
We now know that this was not nearly as nutritious as they supposed it to be.

00:03:08.400 --> 00:03:30.840
That and other vitamin deficiencies contributed to the disaster that was to come.

00:03:30.840 --> 00:03:35.560
As they waited, they knew that farther along the coast, the Norwegian Amundsen and his

00:03:35.560 --> 00:03:40.280
team were waiting to try and beat them to the Pole.

00:03:40.280 --> 00:03:45.320
On November the 1st, at the beginning of the summer, Scott and four companions left this

00:03:45.320 --> 00:03:52.000
hut and set off on the 800 mile march to the South Pole.

00:03:52.000 --> 00:03:55.600
They wore clothes of wool and cotton like these.

00:03:55.600 --> 00:04:00.200
They traveled on long wooden skis with simple bindings.

00:04:00.200 --> 00:04:05.800
And they transported their equipment and food on sledges which they pulled themselves having

00:04:05.800 --> 00:04:10.840
decided against the dogs which Amundsen, their Norwegian rival, was using.

00:04:10.840 --> 00:04:17.320
They reached the Pole on the 17th of January only to find that Amundsen had got there 34

00:04:17.320 --> 00:04:19.160
days before them.

00:04:19.160 --> 00:04:24.040
On the way back they encountered dreadful weather, ran short of supplies and they died

00:04:24.040 --> 00:04:30.440
in their tent of starvation and exhaustion 11 miles from a food depot and less than 100

00:04:30.440 --> 00:04:35.800
miles from the safety of this hut.

00:04:35.800 --> 00:04:40.240
Today some 80 years later a great deal has changed.

00:04:40.240 --> 00:04:44.400
Modern fabrics keep you warm during the worsted conditions.

00:04:44.400 --> 00:04:48.080
Satellites in the sky make communication and navigation easy.

00:04:48.080 --> 00:04:52.680
And almost every day during the summer an aircraft takes off from the ice near here

00:04:52.680 --> 00:05:04.280
and flies directly to the Pole.

00:05:04.280 --> 00:05:12.000
Captain Scott marched for 79 exhausting, back-breaking days before he reached the Pole.

00:05:12.000 --> 00:05:17.160
This plane will make exactly the same journey in less than three hours.

00:05:17.160 --> 00:05:23.280
And today alone there are four other flights like it.

00:05:23.280 --> 00:05:28.420
As you fly along Scott's route it's not only the sheer distance that impresses you, it's

00:05:28.420 --> 00:05:31.900
also the appalling difficulties of the terrain.

00:05:31.900 --> 00:05:36.960
To begin with Scott used a combination of motor-sledge, ponies and dogs.

00:05:36.960 --> 00:05:40.400
But after 409 miles he abandoned them all.

00:05:40.400 --> 00:05:46.720
Thereafter he and his men hauled the sledges themselves, each man pulling a weight of 90

00:05:46.720 --> 00:05:51.040
pounds.

00:05:51.040 --> 00:05:55.920
The decision not to use dogs throughout was probably their undoing.

00:05:55.920 --> 00:06:01.240
Amundsen by doing so made the journey much more quickly and with much less physical effort.

00:06:01.240 --> 00:06:05.560
So when Scott and his companions reached the Pole they found Amundsen's abandoned

00:06:05.560 --> 00:06:07.320
tent already there.

00:06:07.320 --> 00:06:13.200
And inside it a note for Scott to deliver to the King of Norway should Amundsen himself

00:06:13.200 --> 00:06:22.120
fail to return.

00:06:22.120 --> 00:06:28.440
Scott when he arrived at this exact spot and found the Norwegian flag already planted by

00:06:28.440 --> 00:06:35.440
Amundsen wrote in his journal, great God this is an awful place.

00:06:35.440 --> 00:06:41.740
And so it must have been to those five exhausted, bitterly disappointed men with the dreadful

00:06:41.740 --> 00:06:44.280
return journeys still ahead of them.

00:06:44.280 --> 00:06:52.960
Today some 80 years later neither explorer would recognise the place.

00:06:52.960 --> 00:06:59.880
This summer over a hundred scientists and support staff will live and work protected

00:06:59.880 --> 00:07:04.680
from the worst of the weather by this dome.

00:07:04.680 --> 00:07:10.160
Beneath it are smaller insulated buildings for the dome by itself is not sufficient protection

00:07:10.160 --> 00:07:13.240
from the cold.

00:07:13.240 --> 00:07:15.680
It stands 16 metres high.

00:07:15.680 --> 00:07:21.960
It's like a space station, an isolated capsule floating on slowly moving ice nearly 3000

00:07:21.960 --> 00:07:26.720
metres above sea level.

00:07:26.720 --> 00:07:33.400
All supplies for the Pole station have to be brought in by air.

00:07:33.400 --> 00:07:37.760
Even in summer it's so cold that the supply aircraft after they've landed have to keep

00:07:37.760 --> 00:07:44.120
their engines running to stop them from freezing.

00:07:44.120 --> 00:07:49.140
The fuel they bring is transferred into vast bladders which when full will last the station

00:07:49.140 --> 00:07:54.840
through the winter.

00:07:54.840 --> 00:07:59.520
The South Pole is the best place on earth to observe the heavens above.

00:07:59.520 --> 00:08:05.020
The atmosphere is totally clear and free from pollution and the stars don't disappear below

00:08:05.020 --> 00:08:20.720
the horizon as they do elsewhere so that they can be observed continuously.

00:08:20.720 --> 00:08:24.600
Working in Antarctica demands a special kind of scientist.

00:08:24.600 --> 00:08:29.000
You may have the most brilliant mind but that may be of little use if you can't pitch a

00:08:29.000 --> 00:08:46.440
tent or restart a diesel engine.

00:08:46.440 --> 00:08:50.480
Most of the stations are built on the edge of the continent like the Australian base at

00:08:50.480 --> 00:09:01.480
Mawson.

00:09:01.480 --> 00:09:05.040
Here there are other living creatures with which to share your life.

00:09:05.040 --> 00:09:15.040
35 miles from Mawson there are Emperor Penguins which also, like you, will sit out the winter.

00:09:15.040 --> 00:09:26.120
When the last supply ships have left the wintering crews will see no other human being for six

00:09:26.120 --> 00:09:30.720
whole months, perhaps more.

00:09:30.720 --> 00:09:35.120
They must now find a way of living together in a place where, for some of the time, there

00:09:35.120 --> 00:09:38.600
will be no morning, no evening and no escape.

00:09:38.600 --> 00:09:44.520
A routine is all important and there's plenty to do, not only scientific work but all the

00:09:44.520 --> 00:09:47.760
jobs necessary to keep the station running.

00:09:47.760 --> 00:09:50.920
Looking after the dogs is a much sought after job.

00:09:50.920 --> 00:09:57.240
It's refreshing to see living things other than human beings.

00:09:57.240 --> 00:10:02.200
Food becomes hugely important and the cook is one of the most critically watched members

00:10:02.200 --> 00:10:21.560
of the community.

00:10:21.560 --> 00:10:29.440
Most bases have at least a year's supply of food in reserve in case of emergencies.

00:10:29.440 --> 00:10:34.800
And most also have a building away from these living quarters, fully stocked with food in

00:10:34.800 --> 00:10:39.040
case of the worst disaster of all, a fire.

00:10:39.040 --> 00:10:44.160
For no human beings without shelter in conditions like this could survive for more than a few

00:10:44.160 --> 00:10:48.600
hours.

00:10:48.600 --> 00:10:54.360
As winter advances and the days shorten, the sun skims closer to the horizon and eventually

00:10:54.360 --> 00:10:55.880
drops below it.

00:10:55.880 --> 00:11:05.360
Now there will be little or no sunlight whatever for 37 days.

00:11:05.360 --> 00:11:06.360
Midwinter day.

00:11:06.360 --> 00:11:12.840
On Mawson Base, as on every other, it's marked with a great party.

00:11:12.840 --> 00:11:26.360
Entertainments that have been practiced for weeks in secret are now performed in public.

00:11:26.360 --> 00:11:55.940
Why don't we all sing!

00:11:55.940 --> 00:12:01.940
But on the other side, the darkness is broken only by one of nature's most extraordinary spectacles.

00:12:01.940 --> 00:12:27.940
The southern lights, the aurora australis.

00:12:27.940 --> 00:12:33.940
As the sun returns, so do the Adelie penguins.

00:12:33.940 --> 00:12:37.940
They come to one of their traditional colonies only a mile from Mawson Base.

00:12:37.940 --> 00:12:40.940
It's now one of the best studied of all.

00:12:40.940 --> 00:12:45.940
A wire-fenced corridor with an electronic beam across it ensures that some of the birds,

00:12:45.940 --> 00:12:51.940
as they go to and from the sea, are automatically counted and weighed.

00:12:51.940 --> 00:13:15.940
But a few must still be caught and measured in detail to keep a check on the colony's progress.

00:13:15.940 --> 00:13:21.940
Some are given prominent markings so that they can be identified among their near-identical companions,

00:13:21.940 --> 00:13:26.940
even at a distance.

00:13:26.940 --> 00:13:30.940
It is, it must be said, rather disfiguring, but it will disappear at the next molt,

00:13:30.940 --> 00:13:46.940
and it hasn't lessened the affection of the bird's partner.

00:13:46.940 --> 00:13:51.940
Dogs have been used here since Amundsen's day, but dogs are ecological aliens,

00:13:51.940 --> 00:13:54.940
and it's been decided that they must go.

00:13:54.940 --> 00:13:55.940
Many regret that.

00:13:55.940 --> 00:14:00.940
Dogs are great companions, and they can detect one of the major hazards of Antarctic travel,

00:14:00.940 --> 00:14:05.940
a snow-covered crevasse, and stop before they all fall in.

00:14:05.940 --> 00:14:09.940
No motorised sledge can do that.

00:14:09.940 --> 00:14:13.940
This team will be sent to Minnesota in the United States.

00:14:13.940 --> 00:14:24.940
Its departure will mark the end of a great chapter in the short history of mankind in the Antarctic.

00:14:24.940 --> 00:14:32.940
They will be replaced by motorised quikes.

00:14:32.940 --> 00:14:35.940
There's a limit to the amount of fuel such vehicles can carry,

00:14:35.940 --> 00:14:43.940
so they can't cover such great distances as a dog team, but they do travel faster.

00:14:43.940 --> 00:14:47.940
It used to take two days with dogs to reach Mawson's Emperor Colony.

00:14:47.940 --> 00:14:50.940
Now it's only a three-hour drive.

00:14:50.940 --> 00:15:05.940
All year, even throughout the winter, scientists visit this colony to monitor its progress as part of a long-term study.

00:15:05.940 --> 00:15:25.940
There's a serious scientific purpose behind this rugby tackling.

00:15:25.940 --> 00:15:31.940
The bird is to be fitted with a transmitter that will send regular signals by way of an orbiting satellite

00:15:31.940 --> 00:15:35.940
to a monitoring station in Tasmania.

00:15:35.940 --> 00:15:45.940
It, too, is given an identifying mark.

00:15:45.940 --> 00:15:51.940
If this bird is like others, it's now setting off on a 100-mile march to reach open water,

00:15:51.940 --> 00:15:57.940
and when it gets there, it will dive to an astonishing depth of 450 metres to catch fish

00:15:57.940 --> 00:16:03.940
and all the time be recording information to say where it is.

00:16:03.940 --> 00:16:09.940
Hundreds of miles to the north, a grey-headed albatross is providing similar information.

00:16:09.940 --> 00:16:15.940
It, too, has a transmitter on its back which has revealed exactly where it collected the food in its stomach

00:16:15.940 --> 00:16:23.940
that it's now bringing back to its hungry chick.

00:16:23.940 --> 00:16:29.940
It belongs to a colony that has been studied for the past 15 years by a British team.

00:16:29.940 --> 00:16:36.940
The old method of weighing birds was with a simple spring balance.

00:16:36.940 --> 00:16:39.940
But now the researchers use a new device.

00:16:39.940 --> 00:16:47.940
The scales are electronic and concealed inside a fibreglass nest.

00:16:47.940 --> 00:16:54.940
From now on, there will be no need to manhandle the chick just to get its weight.

00:16:54.940 --> 00:16:58.940
The scales transmit a reading every 10 minutes to a nearby hut

00:16:58.940 --> 00:17:03.940
where there is a watching scientist and recording apparatus.

00:17:03.940 --> 00:17:12.940
This shows that one of the parents brings 500 grams of squid, fish, lamprey and krill to the chick every three days.

00:17:12.940 --> 00:17:30.940
And signals from the satellite have revealed that the adult has travelled several hundred miles in the process of doing so.

00:17:30.940 --> 00:17:36.940
To film this series, we drew heavily on the discoveries made by scientific teams all over the continent.

00:17:36.940 --> 00:17:45.940
Guided by their satellite data, we aimed, among other things, to record in pictures just what those albatross and penguins did in the open ocean.

00:17:45.940 --> 00:17:51.940
To do that involved developing cameras and lenses that could cope with these hostile conditions

00:17:51.940 --> 00:17:56.940
and finding cameramen who could cope with them too.

00:17:56.940 --> 00:18:01.940
Swimming in the open ocean in the near-freezing seas may be second nature to an albatross,

00:18:01.940 --> 00:18:09.940
but it's a daring thing for a cameraman to do.

00:18:09.940 --> 00:18:19.940
The reward for him is sights that have never been filmed before.

00:18:19.940 --> 00:18:26.940
On board our ice-strengthened vessel, the Able Jay, we carried boats, diving gear and video apparatus.

00:18:26.940 --> 00:18:38.940
As well as freediving cameramen, we had remotely controlled cameras mounted on the inflatables.

00:18:38.940 --> 00:18:51.940
One of our priorities was to find a swarm of krill. After weeks of searching, we did.

00:18:51.940 --> 00:19:01.940
And so had a pair of humpback whales.

00:19:01.940 --> 00:19:08.940
The remotely controlled video cameras gave us unique pictures.

00:19:08.940 --> 00:19:14.940
They recorded in unparalleled detail the whole of the whale's fishing technique.

00:19:14.940 --> 00:19:34.940
From the moment they released their curtain of bubbles, hemming in and concentrating the krill, to the final catch.

00:19:34.940 --> 00:19:40.940
We also had another vessel, a small steel-hulled yacht, the Damien II.

00:19:40.940 --> 00:19:45.940
She had a retractable keel, so she could operate in waters only a metre deep,

00:19:45.940 --> 00:19:58.940
and go into shallow bays where no other vessel had been before.

00:19:58.940 --> 00:20:02.940
Jérôme Ponce is the skipper and owner of the Damien.

00:20:02.940 --> 00:20:09.940
With his biologist wife, he's spent ten seasons exploring every cove and bay on the Antarctic Peninsula,

00:20:09.940 --> 00:20:27.940
and knows them in a way no one else does.

00:20:27.940 --> 00:20:33.940
He was able to land camera teams on tiny remote and uninhabited islands.

00:20:33.940 --> 00:20:42.940
Each night, a radio hook-up linked all the camps and the ships, which were often separated by several hundred miles of ice or ocean.

00:20:42.940 --> 00:20:46.940
Able J, this is Bailey Head, reading you loud and clear, loud and clear.

00:20:46.940 --> 00:20:47.940
Hermy, over.

00:20:47.940 --> 00:20:57.940
Bailey Head, Able J, to confirm your message, two tents cameras damaged, one tent broken pole, all OK. Over.

00:20:57.940 --> 00:21:00.940
A camera on a jib arm.

00:21:00.940 --> 00:21:09.940
It gives a splendid high angle view of a penguin colony and enables you to move alongside an individual penguin on its perambulations.

00:21:09.940 --> 00:21:26.940
But the whole thing weighs 120 kilos, and carrying that around over snowfields and rocky cliffs reduces even the strongest camera team to gasping wrecks.

00:21:26.940 --> 00:21:34.940
To get unbumpy pictures on the move, cameraman Paul Atkins used a special mountain harness called a Steadicam.

00:21:34.940 --> 00:21:58.940
That way he was able to move smoothly into really close quarters with such tricky and dangerous subjects as fighting fur seals.

00:21:58.940 --> 00:22:10.940
Blizzards often brought land based operations to a halt, but even in such conditions as this, there was work that could be done underwater, if you can dig out the air cylinders from under the snow.

00:22:10.940 --> 00:22:17.940
Diving under the ice is very different from doing so in the open ocean, as cameraman Mike Degree explains.

00:22:17.940 --> 00:22:23.940
I'm generally a fair weather diver. I like warm weather, sunshine, palm trees and hammocks.

00:22:23.940 --> 00:22:28.940
I jumped into a seal hole, pushing the ice away as I entered, and they handed me my camera.

00:22:28.940 --> 00:22:35.940
Surprisingly, I wasn't too cold, except around where my mouth held on to my regulator, and that instantly froze and became numb.

00:22:35.940 --> 00:22:46.940
Suddenly everything was quiet, and I found myself looking at, easily, one of the most extraordinary scenes I have ever, ever experienced.

00:22:46.940 --> 00:22:53.940
And I dropped down through a hole in the ice. I was completely surrounded by ice, a tunnel maybe 20 feet across.

00:22:53.940 --> 00:23:11.940
Everything above me on the land was roaring with wind, and down there there was absolutely no sound, except for the distant trills of Weddell seals.

00:23:11.940 --> 00:23:21.940
Weddell seal researcher Amal Ajmi works underwater too, but she doesn't get wet.

00:23:21.940 --> 00:23:27.940
She makes her observations from a capsule suspended 10 meters down beneath the ice.

00:23:27.940 --> 00:23:35.940
From there, she records the sounds of the seals, while noting on a tape recorder the details of their movements.

00:23:35.940 --> 00:23:43.940
There's a lot of activity, a lot.

00:23:43.940 --> 00:23:50.940
There are a pair right next to the hydrophone. Those are probably the loudest animals.

00:23:50.940 --> 00:24:11.940
There's one single seal that is on my left, and it seems to be watching the mother and pup that were near the hydrophone.

00:24:11.940 --> 00:24:16.940
Other researchers have been studying a colony of emperor penguins for many years.

00:24:16.940 --> 00:24:29.940
They watch them underwater from within a protective cage, for where there are lots of penguins, you can expect to find dangerous penguin hunters, leopard seals or killer whales.

00:24:29.940 --> 00:24:39.940
And this is a leopard seal, a huge animal, nearly four meters long.

00:24:39.940 --> 00:24:57.940
A remotely controlled camera, properly placed, will record the exit of the fleeing penguins.

00:24:57.940 --> 00:25:26.940
But even out of water, they're not out of danger. Another leopard seal waits for them.

00:25:26.940 --> 00:25:37.940
Many people reckon that the leopard seal is the most dangerous killer in Antarctic waters, and that it would be suicide to get in the water with one.

00:25:37.940 --> 00:25:48.940
But the camera team were determined to film them hunting without the encumbrance of a cage. Peter Schoonz and Doug Allen were the first to try.

00:25:48.940 --> 00:26:01.940
I've been underwater with all the other species of southern seals, so I had this feeling that leopard seals weren't actually going to attack us, at least not without some warning.

00:26:01.940 --> 00:26:19.940
Pete Schoonz and I thought we could recognise if their behaviour ever did slip over the borderline from curiosity to aggression.

00:26:19.940 --> 00:26:37.940
It definitely produces a fair rush of adrenaline when a 12-foot seal comes out of the hazy distance and ends up almost taking the entire front of the camera into its mouth.

00:26:37.940 --> 00:26:46.940
You have to feel sorry for the young penguins. They just don't stand a chance. It's like a cat with a mouse.

00:26:46.940 --> 00:26:58.940
And here I was, the cat owner being presented with the prey.

00:26:58.940 --> 00:27:09.940
But I shouldn't deny the sheer excitement of filming so intimately one of Antarctica's top predators.

00:27:09.940 --> 00:27:25.940
This drama is a symbol of Antarctica and I'll always count myself privileged to have seen it.

00:27:25.940 --> 00:27:47.940
It's still less than a century since the first man set foot on the Antarctic continent. Yet today hundreds of scientists live and work here, winter and summer, increasing numbers of tourists arrive, and every year modern technologies make it increasingly easy for people to survive here.

00:27:47.940 --> 00:28:00.940
Despite all that, there are still very few footsteps in the Antarctic snow. Mining has been banned for a further 50 years and the Antarctic Treaty remains relatively effective.

00:28:00.940 --> 00:28:28.940
At a time when it's possible for 30 people to stand on the top of Everest in one day, Antarctica still remains a remote, lonely and desolate continent. A place where it's possible to see the splendors and immensities of the natural world at its most dramatic and much more witness them almost exactly as they were long, long before human beings ever arrived on the surface of this planet.

00:28:28.940 --> 00:28:31.940
Long may it remain so.
